WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.741
Every week, new A.I. tools appear.

2
00:00:03.741 --> 00:00:08.694
Skills, models, scanners, agents. Each one promises better work.

3
00:00:08.694 --> 00:00:11.924
We collected a hundred and twenty-five of them.

4
00:00:11.924 --> 00:00:17.806
So which ones actually work? Which are safe? And which fit the project in front of you?

5
00:00:18.206 --> 00:00:21.891
Our answer is a grade, and only proof can raise it.

6
00:00:21.891 --> 00:00:25.331
There are six steps, from E zero to E five.

7
00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:28.238
E zero: we have only read about it.

8
00:00:28.238 --> 00:00:31.424
E one: it did real work in a project.

9
00:00:31.424 --> 00:00:35.589
E two: it was checked against a result we wrote down first.

10
00:00:35.589 --> 00:00:41.719
The higher steps need many test cases, a second reviewer, and time in production.

11
00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:45.177
Before anything is installed, it is scanned.

12
00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:52.365
The scanner reads the files, looking for stolen passwords, hidden instructions, or code pulled from the internet.

13
00:00:52.365 --> 00:00:57.332
Each finding comes with a severity and a line number, so a person can check it.

14
00:00:57.332 --> 00:01:07.363
In twenty-four of fifty-one scans, at least one part was not safe. Those parts are not installed unless a person reads the report and decides.

15
00:01:07.363 --> 00:01:18.294
We tested the scanner too, with traps we planted. It rated two of five as only a caution, so the ledger now blocks any high-risk finding until a person reads it.

16
00:01:18.694 --> 00:01:22.904
A grade only moves when a tool is used in a real project.

17
00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:28.323
Each use is written down: the task, what broke, what was fixed, and the evidence.

18
00:01:28.323 --> 00:01:37.437
F F mpeg measured the first draft of this video and found its sound too quiet. We fixed it, and F F mpeg moved up to E two.

19
00:01:37.437 --> 00:01:45.260
A hundred and four tools have no successful use in the ledger yet. They stay on file, as candidates and references.

20
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:49.866
Of the twenty-one that have, eighteen are at E one or higher.

21
00:01:50.266 --> 00:01:54.320
Here is one tool, start to finish: the voice you are hearing.

22
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.795
It entered the ledger with no proof, at E zero.

23
00:01:57.795 --> 00:02:03.528
The scan found no known problems, and its model files matched their published fingerprints.

24
00:02:03.528 --> 00:02:09.326
Then it recorded the first narration for this video. That real use proposed E one.

25
00:02:09.326 --> 00:02:12.576
A person reviewed the proposal and approved it.

26
00:02:12.576 --> 00:02:17.840
To reach E two, it needs a check against a result written down first.

27
00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:24.784
When a new project starts, the ledger suggests the tools that fit it, and how each one will be tested there.

28
00:02:24.784 --> 00:02:29.378
Every new use adds proof, so the next project starts from what was learned.

29
00:02:29.378 --> 00:02:32.190
The ledger also learns as it goes.

30
00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:42.726
Every problem is written down with its fix: seventy-seven found, sixty-six fixed. And a failed check can become a new rule, like the stricter scan rule.

31
00:02:43.126 --> 00:02:49.274
A hundred and twenty-five tools, eighty-five uses, twenty-one decisions. All on record.

32
00:02:49.274 --> 00:02:52.368
This is the Tool Research Ledger.
